WEBVTT

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.779
An image gradient is two numbers per
pixel:

00:00:02.779 --> 00:00:07.192
how fast brightness changes to the right,
and how fast it changes downwards.

00:00:07.192 --> 00:00:12.402
The Sobel filter takes a difference across
three rows and averages them with weights
one,

00:00:12.403 --> 00:00:12.930
two, one.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:16.655
On a ramp that climbs twelve per pixel
across and five down,

00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:18.768
its sums are ninety-six and forty.

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:22.067
Divide by eight, and you get exactly
twelve and five.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:25.854
So the edge strength is thirteen, at about
twenty-three degrees.

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:30.667
A difference amplifies noise, because
noise is the highest frequency there is.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:33.617
Here is a plain difference on a noisy
image.

00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:37.893
The principled fix is to blur with a
Gaussian, then differentiate,

00:00:37.894 --> 00:00:39.643
and one kernel can do both.

00:00:39.642 --> 00:00:44.204
It separates into a derivative along the
rows and a blur along the columns,

00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:47.732
so a thirteen-tap kernel needs twenty-six
multiplications,

00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:49.619
not one hundred and sixty-nine.

00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:54.029
A good feature point is one whose patch
would look different if it moved in any
direction.

00:00:54.029 --> 00:00:57.166
So shift a window, and add up the squared
differences.

00:00:57.166 --> 00:01:00.791
On a flat patch nothing changes, and the
map stays dark.

00:01:00.791 --> 00:01:04.466
On an edge, moving along it changes
nothing, so you get a valley.

00:01:04.466 --> 00:01:07.754
At a corner, every direction costs, and
you get a bowl.

00:01:07.754 --> 00:01:10.900
A Taylor expansion turns this into a
quadratic form,

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:13.442
with the structure tensor M in the middle.

00:01:13.374 --> 00:01:16.162
Two pixels both have the gradient one,
one.

00:01:16.162 --> 00:01:19.374
Every entry of M is two, which looks big
everywhere.

00:01:19.374 --> 00:01:21.262
Is this a corner, or an edge?

00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:23.649
Pause and work out the eigenvalues.

00:01:25.098 --> 00:01:25.998
It's an edge.

00:01:25.998 --> 00:01:28.198
The eigenvalues are four and zero:

00:01:28.198 --> 00:01:31.036
the same edge, turned by forty-five
degrees.

00:01:31.036 --> 00:01:31.936
Both small:

00:01:31.936 --> 00:01:32.586
flat.

00:01:32.586 --> 00:01:33.223
One large:

00:01:33.223 --> 00:01:33.961
an edge.

00:01:33.961 --> 00:01:34.798
Both large:

00:01:34.798 --> 00:01:35.761
a corner.

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:39.273
Harris avoids the square roots with the
determinant and the trace.

00:01:39.273 --> 00:01:43.261
The corner scores three point three six,
and edges score negative.

00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:47.773
Keep the strongest local maxima, and these
are the corners of a real image.
